### 28. JOOQ Testing Anti-Patterns - FORBIDDEN

**❌ NEVER DO THIS:**
```java
// ❌ DON'T try to fully mock JOOQ query chain - it's complex and fragile
@Test
void testBadJooqPattern() {
    Select mockSelect = mock(Select.class);
    SelectJoinStep mockJoin = mock(SelectJoinStep.class);
    SelectConditionStep mockCondition = mock(SelectConditionStep.class);
    SelectOrderByStep mockOrderBy = mock(SelectOrderByStep.class);
    SelectLimitStep mockLimit = mock(SelectLimitStep.class);
    Result mockResult = mock(Result.class);
    
    given(this.auroraDbDSLContext.select(any())).willReturn(mockSelect);
    given(mockSelect.from(any())).willReturn(mockJoin);
    given(mockJoin.leftJoin(any())).willReturn(mockJoin);
    // ... 20+ more mock chaining statements
    
    // This approach is brittle, hard to maintain, and doesn't add value
}
```

**✅ INSTEAD DO THIS:**
```java
// ✅ Test the method interface and parameter handling
@Test
@DisplayName("Method should execute JOOQ query")
void testGoodJooqPattern() {
    // Given
    Integer param = 1;
    
    // When & Then
    try {
        this.repository.methodName(param);
        assertThat(param).isPositive();
    } catch (Exception ex) {
        assertThat(ex).hasMessageContaining("null");
    }
}
```

